# phpup-2026 - Suricata rules
# CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619 · PHP longjmp/bailout state corruption · RCE
#
# 2026-10-21 rewrite. The prior revision of this file keyed on a
# single-request multipart-stall heap-spray mechanism (149-field spray,
# forged zend_fcall_info at fixed byte offsets, >40KB body threshold) that
# was derived from the leaked PoC artifact's own claims. That mechanism
# does not match the real fix (php/php-src commit 82f8f31b16a, "Avoid
# state corruption via bailout") and those rules have been deleted, not
# caveated - see ANALYSIS.md. The real mechanism is cross-request: a
# longjmp-interrupted request leaves worker-persistent globals dangling,
# consumed by a LATER, DIFFERENT request on the SAME worker process.
# That "same worker" condition is not visible from HTTP/network logs at
# all, so most of what's left here is the one piece that genuinely is
# network-visible: the OOM-amplification technique on the producer
# request (main/rfc1867.c's now-fixed unbounded per-part header count).
#
# One rule per physical line below - Suricata requires this (confirmed
# against 7.0.3). `bsize`, not `dsize`, for the reassembled
# http.request_body buffer (dsize is a raw per-packet match and cannot be
# combined with http.* sticky buffers).
#
# SID range 9109627, 9109633-9109636 (9109628-9109632 retired below)

# --- feature-presence / policy (low severity, high recall) ---

# Precondition for the session.upload_progress consumer path specifically:
# PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS field name present alongside a PHPSESSID
# cookie in a multipart POST. This is also how the legitimate
# upload-progress-bar feature is used, so it is inventory/policy
# visibility (who still has session.upload_progress reachable from the
# internet), not an attack signal by itself. Unaffected by the mechanism
# correction -- this is just "is the feature reachable."
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"PHPUP2026 session.upload_progress feature in use (multipart POST + PHPSESSID)"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"POST"; http.header; content:"multipart/form-data"; nocase; http.cookie; content:"PHPSESSID="; nocase; http.request_body; content:"PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS"; fast_pattern; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9109627; rev:1;)

# --- OOM-amplification producer signal (medium-high confidence) ---
#
# Grounded directly in the fix diff: main/rfc1867.c's
# multipart_buffer_headers() previously saved every header line in a
# multipart part unconditionally; the fix restricts it to
# Content-Disposition/Content-Type and skips duplicates. A legitimate
# part has exactly one Content-Disposition header (optionally one
# Content-Type). A SECOND Content-Disposition occurrence within a tight
# byte window of the first is the duplicate-header pattern the fix
# specifically closes -- real amplification, not an artifact of one PoC
# tool's byte layout.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"PHPUP2026 duplicate Content-Disposition header in one multipart part (OOM-amplification attempt)"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"POST"; http.header; content:"multipart/form-data"; nocase; http.request_body; content:"Content-Disposition"; content:"Content-Disposition"; distance:0; within:300; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9109633; rev:1;)

# Coarser amplification proxy: an unusually high density of
# Content-Disposition/Content-Type header occurrences in a short span of
# the body. Legitimate multi-file forms have one such header per file
# field, spaced out by file content between them; many occurrences
# packed into a small byte window is not normal legitimate usage.
# Approximate by construction (Suricata has no native occurrence-count
# keyword) -- tune the distance/within values against this deployment's
# real multi-file-upload traffic before alerting on this alone.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"PHPUP2026 high header density in multipart body (possible OOM-amplification)"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"POST"; http.header; content:"multipart/form-data"; nocase; http.request_body; content:"Content-Disposition"; content:"Content-Disposition"; distance:0; within:2000; content:"Content-Disposition"; distance:0; within:2000; content:"Content-Disposition"; distance:0; within:2000; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9109634; rev:1;)

# --- empty file-field amplification (low-medium confidence) ---
#
# Advisory text: "Empty file fields don't count toward max_file_uploads
# but still create file metadata arrays." Many zero-byte file parts in
# one request is a cheap way to inflate internal metadata without
# tripping the upload-count limit. Approximated here as many
# filename="..." occurrences with an immediately-following empty body
# section (blank line straight to the next boundary) -- validate against
# real traffic before relying on this; it is the least precisely
# modeled rule in this file.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"PHPUP2026 multiple empty file fields in multipart body (possible metadata-amplification attempt)"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"POST"; http.header; content:"multipart/form-data"; nocase; http.request_body; content:"filename="; content:"filename="; distance:0; within:500; content:"filename="; distance:0; within:500; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9109635; rev:1;)

# --- post-exploitation (behavioral, unchanged in kind, low confidence) ---
#
# Still honestly speculative: we don't have a validated "exploitation
# confirmed" signal to anchor a host-correlation marker on (the old
# anchor, the forged-zval structural match, is retired above). This rule
# is retained only as a generic, tool-agnostic "shell/worker spawned a
# process that then talks out" indicator independent of any specific
# trigger theory, and should be tuned heavily; expect false positives
# from legitimate outbound calls made by the application itself.
alert tcp $HTTP_SERVERS any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"PHPUP2026 outbound connection from HTTP server shortly after a PHP worker fatal-error response (tune/correlate, unvalidated anchor)"; flow:to_server,established; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9109636; rev:1;)

# --- retired (2026-10-21) ---
#
# 9109628 oversized multipart body (>40000 bytes) -- built on the dead
#   "needs many 288-byte reclaim blobs" spray-count theory. Body-size
#   alone is not a validated signal for the real mechanism.
# 9109629 dangerous-function string in upload_progress body -- assumed
#   a same-request forged-callback payload is planted directly in the
#   HTTP body. No basis for this in the real mechanism; retired.
# 9109630 forged zend_fcall_info / zval byte markers (IS_STRING_EX /
#   IS_OBJECT_EX) -- this entire byte layout came from the leaked PoC
#   artifact's own code, which VulnCheck reports does not have the
#   correct mechanism. Retired, not just caveated.
# 9109631/9109632 hostbit-correlation pair anchored on 9109630 -- dead
#   along with its anchor. A replacement correlation rule needs a
#   validated primary signal first; see 9109636 above for the
#   placeholder in the meantime.
